  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "M.I.Sunny vs State Of Kerala on 27 October, 2020 In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present The Honourable Mr.Justice N.Anil Kumar Tuesday, The 27Th Day Of October 2020/5Th Karthika, 1942 Crl.Rev.Pet.No.1422 Of 2005(C) Against The Judgment In Cra 276/2001 Dated 31-03-2005 Of Additional Sessions Court (Ad Hoc)-I, Kottayam St 14/1995 Of Chief Judicial Magistrate Court, Kottayam Dated 23-05-2001 Revision Petitioner/Appellant/Accused No.1: M.I.Sunny, S/O.M.I.Itty, Madavasseril, Thiruvarppu, Kottayam. By Adv.Sri.P.Ravindra Babu Respondent/Respondent/Complainant: State Of Kerala, Represented By Food Inspector, Kottayam Municipality, Represented By Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. By Senior Public Prosecutor Sri.M.S.Breez This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 22-10-2020, The Court On 27-10-2020 Passed The Following: Crl.R.P.No.1422 of 2005 ..2..",
    "facts": "The revision petitioner was the 1st accused in S.T.No.14/1995 on the file of the Chief Judicial Magistrate Court, Kottayam and the appellant in Crl.A.No.276/2001. The original complaint was filed by the Food Inspector, Kottayam Municipality against the accused alleging commission of offences punishable under Sections 2(ia) (a) (h) (j) (1) 7(i) 16(1)(a)(i) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act,1954 (hereinafter referred to as 'the PFA Act') and Rules 24 and 28 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules. 2. The prosecution case in brief is that on 19.12.1994 at about 2.30 pm. the Food Inspector, ..3.. Kottayam Municipality purchased three icing cakes of 500 gms each from the shop room of the petitioner in accordance with rules. One of the samples was sent for analysis and the public analyst, on analysis, reported that the sample contained non-permitted coal tar dye, Rhodamine B and therefore adulterated. Hence, the complaint was filed. 3. The learned Magistrate took cognizance of the offence and issued summons to the accused. On the appearance of the accused, particulars of the offence were read over to him to which he pleaded not guilty. 4. During the trial, the accused filed an application to implead the 2nd accused, the manufacturer of the icing cakes. Accordingly, the 2nd accused was impleaded. His presence could not be procured despite various coercive steps against him. Hence, he was declared as an absconder during trial. .. 4.. 5. When the case came up for evidence PWs.1 and 2 were examined and marked Exts.P1 to P16 on prosecution side. DWs.1 and 2 were examined and marked Ext.D1 on the side of the accused. The accused challenged the judgment in appeal. Hence, the revision petitioner has preferred this revision before this Court. 6. Heard both sides. 7. The prosecution was initiated based on Ext.P12 Form-III report received from the Public Analyst. Ext.P14 Form-II report confirmed the finding in Ext.P12.",
    "arguments": "In ..5.. Pepsico India Holdings Pvt.Ltd. v. Food Inspector and another [2011 (1) SCC 176], the Apex Court held that the Laboratories where samples of articles of food or adulterants may be analysed by the public analyst is in accordance with Section 23(1A)(ee) and 23(1A)(hh) of the PFA Act.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "In ..5.. Pepsico India Holdings Pvt.Ltd. v. Food Inspector and another [2011 (1) SCC 176], the Apex Court held that the Laboratories where samples of articles of food or adulterants may be analysed by the public analyst is in accordance with Section 23(1A)(ee) and 23(1A)(hh) of the PFA Act."
  },
